{"author":{"id":"609bf866d4a5d716c19faf5d617b3bf3de478230d29602d72aad5aa90a80466f"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1794731176,"nonce":"i/eTU3NrRMUhpq02hcwHv55r+as=","message":"NUC timing of the native writer (2026-11-15, Linux x86_64, Haxe 4.3.6 built from source with OCaml 5.1.1, plugin 9370bcf2). Unit suite, one pinned core, 9 interleaved reps, medians [min..max]:\n\n- typing, official haxe 4.3.6: 2813 ms [2683..3184]\n- typing, own haxe: 2221 ms [2147..2403]\n- own haxe + native export: 2652 ms [2607..2789], +431 ms over typing\n- own haxe + Haxe macro exporter: 5200 ms [5051..5619], +2978 ms over typing\n\nThe native writer costs about a seventh of the macro exporter. Same program on Linux: tast_dump --against, 8902 functions, missing 0, extra 0, differing 0 (build-date constant allowed).\n\nQuiet-box note: the wrapper waited for two consecutive checks with no foreign compile (another session's cargo test had kept a rustc busy for the first, discarded run: same ordering, +325 ms / +2722 ms, wider spread). The bench script's own NOT-QUIET flag was wrong: its pgrep matched its own shell; fixed to match by process name.\n\nOur own haxe build types the suite about 21% faster than the official 4.3.6 binary under identical conditions (both runs). Cause not investigated (the official binary's build flags and OCaml version are not known here); worth knowing before shipping our own build, since it also means shipping it costs nothing in typing speed.\n\nSetup cost of the NUC toolchain, no sudo: m4, pcre2, mbedtls 2.28 built into ~/local, opam 2.2.1, OCaml 5.1.1, Haxe and the plugin in about 11 minutes at idle priority on one core. Traps: gcc 15 needs CC=\"gcc -std=gnu17\"; the pkg-config stub must report zstd missing or OCaml's configure fails; the NUC has about 2 GB disk free after it.","files":null}]}